# Dates from engine/notation/chronicle.ts: the Rosetta decree 196 BC, the last carved hieroglyphs AD
# 394 (Philae), the stone found 1799, Champollion's reading 1822. The calendar is from Wikipedia's
# Egyptian calendar.
lesson history-egypt-the-river-and-the-stone v=1 format=teach grade=5 unit=6 subject=history levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "Egypt: the river's year and the stone in three scripts"
  goal "Work out the Egyptian year from the Nile's seasons, and see how a stone with one message in three scripts let people read hieroglyphs again."

  look {
    scene 23x9 {
      table t cols=3 head=["Season", "Means", "Roughly"] cells=["Akhet", "flood", "Sep to Jan", "Peret", "fields out", "Jan to May", "Shemu", "harvest", "May to Sep"] at=canvas(0, 0)
    }
    say """
      Ancient Egypt lived by the river Nile. Every year the river flooded and left rich mud on the
      fields, and the Egyptian year followed it in three seasons: Akhet, the flood; Peret, when the
      fields came out of the water; and Shemu, the harvest. Hieroglyphs were last carved in AD 394,
      and then nobody could read them. In 1799 a stone was found at Rosetta with one decree on it
      three times, in hieroglyphs, in Demotic and in Greek. It was made in 196 BC, and in 1822
      Jean-François Champollion showed how the hieroglyphs worked.
      """
  }
  do {
    level easy {
      worked history.egypt-calendar k=0
    }
    show history.egypt-calendar k=2 easy-k=1 hard-k=3
    show history.egypt-season k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
    show history.egypt-order k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
    show history.egypt-between k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
    show history.egypt-stone k=0 hard-k=1
    show history.egypt-scripts k=0 hard-k=1
    show history.egypt-season k=2 easy-k=1 hard-k=3
    show history.egypt-between k=2 easy-k=1 hard-k=3
    level hard {
      show history.egypt-order k=3
    }
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show history.egypt-why k=0 hard-k=1
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show history.egypt-code k=0 hard-k=1
  }
  remember {
    say "The Nile's flood set the Egyptian year, and one decree in three scripts on the Rosetta Stone let hieroglyphs be read again."
  }

  grown-ups """
    The idea is ancient Egypt in depth: a year built on a river, and how a forgotten writing was
    read again. The step up from the first cities of grade three, which met Egypt in passing, is a
    calendar worked from the seasons and the idea of reading an unknown script from the same words
    in a known one. The way in is the days in a season; the core adds the year, names the seasons,
    orders Egypt's dates and counts the years across year one. The two-star question asks why the
    year began with the flood, or why the Greek helped; the three-star question is a made-up stone
    to crack in the same way. The wrong idea to watch for is counting across year one as if there
    were a year nought. If the child is stuck, draw the three seasons as a wheel and write the
    months round it. Off the page, look up the Rosetta Stone on the British Museum's website, where
    it has been on show since 1802. England's key stage 2 history asks for "the achievements of the
    earliest civilizations: an overview of where and when the first civilizations appeared and a
    depth study of one of the following: Ancient Sumer; The Indus Valley; Ancient Egypt; The Shang
    Dynasty of Ancient China"; Russia's history for grade five begins with the ancient world,
    Egypt among it (recalled).
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level a worked example finds the days in a season, the seasons are named from what happens in them, the orders are far apart, and the years counted are both AD."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the calendar's drift is worked out, the season is found from a month, the orders are close together, the years run across year one, and the code has two signs to find."
  }
}
